 2. `ProviderGuard` requires a current fenced run claim. Every time-sensitive database operation reads its clock after acquiring the transaction, so a queued operation cannot authorize work using a timestamp from before its wait. Before provider work it atomically records the stage claim, complete input fingerprint, prepared receipt, submission intent, configured limits, client idempotency key where supported, and worst-case charge reservation. The fingerprint includes owner, document/source, stage/unit, artifact hashes, complete request data, pipeline/schema versions, provider/model/prompt/reasoning, and relevant execution parameters.
 3. A separate committed `dispatching` transition precedes the callback. A repeated delivery cannot invoke that attempt again. The adapter calls `submitted(remoteId)` immediately when the provider supplies its ID. A process loss after dispatch begins and before an ID/response is saved is unknown completion. A fresh fenced claim may adopt a prepared attempt in the same run only after its lease expires and only when no dispatch marker or remote ID exists. The new claim immediately fences the old provider writer.
 4. Responses and their hashes are saved before callers receive them. Raw responses remain pending until the stage validator accepts them. Interrupted local validation reuses the saved bytes without provider work. Reuse applies `validateReceiptReuse` against the expected request and also compares the full configuration and input hashes; row and receipt identities must agree. Pending validation applies the same source, versions, unit, fingerprint, configuration and hash checks before the validator runs. Only an explicitly pending saved response can bypass the completed-accepted requirement for local revalidation; rejected data cannot. Prefer the latest accepted matching response, then pending validation. Corrupt data never becomes successful replay.
-5. Reservations use integer micro-US dollars, including configured OCR costs. Admission checks known actual charges plus every outstanding reservation against both the run cap and the named authorized budget. The independent charge ledger survives document deletion without retaining document payloads. Missing actual charges retain the full reservation even after success. Unknown completion retains its reservation. Only a proven undispatched attempt or a reported actual charge can reduce the reservation. A new budget identity requires a new authorized allowance.
-6. Provider concurrency, maximum output tokens, response bytes, per-call deadline, run deadline, and attempt allowance are checked before dispatch. The guard passes these limits to the adapter and rejects excessive reported output. Adapters apply output limits at the actual provider request. Transport tests and the recorded live integration prove request settings and the separate AmuseMe endpoint; repeat affected checks after a provider or limit change.
-7. Live processing requires explicitly enabled access, a separate HTTPS MinerU endpoint, both provider credentials, a positive authorized spending cap and positive worst-case stage charges. Offline fixtures require an explicitly injected fixture policy. Missing live configuration fails before provider work; the HTTP admission layer must expose the same availability result.
-8. The guard owns paid retries. There are no automatic paid retries. Browser mutations, Mastra step retries, model SDK retries, transport retries and remote job submission retries remain zero. A fresh attempt requires an owner-authorized request persisted with its new run and exact attempt grant, plus acknowledgment of possible repeated work when completion is unknown. Caller strings grant nothing. Complete transient Meta responses may receive an explicit bounded retry; invalid output cannot. Each new attempt reserves its own budget, with earlier charges and uncertain reservations counted across the unchanged document policy. A still-live prior attempt cannot be retried concurrently. Existing MinerU UUIDs recover through GET, never another POST. See [run recovery](run-recovery.md).
+5. Reservations use integer micro-US dollars, including configured OCR costs. Admission checks known actual charges plus every outstanding reservation against both the run cap and the named authorized budget. The independent charge ledger survives document deletion without retaining document payloads. Missing actual charges retain the full reservation even after success. Unknown completion retains its reservation. Only a proven undispatched attempt or a reported actual charge can reduce the reservation. A new budget identity requires a new authorized allowance. The current explicit acceptance allowance is recorded in the [operations runbook](../runbooks/application-operations.md#november-1-reading-guide-acceptance-allowance); the per-document cap remains $30, bounded by the configured processing total. Preserve stage reservations, the original budget ID, historical policies and all charges; configuration does not reset remaining allowance.
+6. Provider concurrency, maximum output tokens, response bytes, per-call deadline, run deadline, and attempt allowance are checked before dispatch. The live policy allows four calls in flight; a run uses at most three at once (figure crop reviews on one page, or figure descriptions), so one stale or operator call does not push a figure into its fallback. Each preparation runs in the serialized storage transaction, so concurrent reservations see each other and never exceed the run cap or the authorized budget. The guard passes these limits to the adapter and rejects excessive reported output. Adapters apply output limits at the actual provider request. Transport tests and the recorded live integration prove request settings and the separate AmuseMe endpoint; repeat affected checks after a provider or limit change.
+7. Live processing requires explicitly enabled access, the Meta credential, a positive authorized spending cap and positive worst-case stage charges. MinerU configuration is optional historical cleanup metadata. Offline fixtures require an explicitly injected fixture policy. Missing live configuration fails before provider work; the HTTP admission layer must expose the same availability result.
+8. The guard owns paid retries. There are no automatic paid retries. Browser mutations, Mastra step retries, model SDK retries, transport retries and remote job submission retries remain zero. A fresh attempt requires an owner-authorized request persisted with its new run and exact attempt grant, plus acknowledgment of possible repeated work when completion is unknown. Caller strings grant nothing. Complete transient Meta responses may receive an explicit bounded retry; invalid output cannot. Invalid output instead gets at most two [Muse review](muse-review.md) attempts with the `review` tag: each references a completed answer for the same unit, counts toward the unit's attempt allowance, and keeps the remaining mandatory reservations free. `ProviderGuard.rejected` returns the saved rejected answer for an exact request so resumed and retried runs replay it. A retry grant applies only to its exact request fingerprint; a unit's other completed requests keep replaying. Retry requests skip completed rejected answers rather than resending them. Each new attempt reserves its own budget, with earlier charges and uncertain reservations counted across the unchanged document policy. A still-live prior attempt cannot be retried concurrently. Existing MinerU UUIDs recover through GET, never another POST. See [run recovery](run-recovery.md).
 9. Run claims renew while the starter executes. Cancellation, a deadline, lost lease or starter failure interrupts the run; it does not resubmit a provider mutation. Provider cancellation after dispatch records unknown completion because local cancellation does not prove remote cancellation. Store failure preserves the durable dispatch intent for reconciliation.
 10. Guard tests use the real local SQLite store and fake Effect provider/starter services. They cover repeated delivery, concurrent claims, process-style close/reopen, prepared and dispatched crash windows, remote-ID persistence, unknown completion, cap exhaustion, missing configuration and limits. Separate integration tests exercise actual adapter serialization, registered Mastra workflows, saved per-page recovery and the retry UI. Live integration evidence is recorded separately; deterministic fixtures do not establish provider availability or content accuracy.
 11. `GuardedResponse.delivery` describes the current invocation, not which run paid for a receipt. Reports must project each original receipt once using its persisted `scope.runId`; a resumed same-run replay still belongs to that run's recorded provider work. Reuse from an earlier run is labeled reused. Do not compute spending from invocation delivery flags or omit charges after replay.
+12. The optional guide model and worst-case reservation preserve old stored configurations and policies. A guide dispatch requires both explicitly; never choose another stage's configuration or default an absent reservation to zero. New normal uploads configure the guide and account for it in mandatory preflight. For historical configurations without a guide, binding, retry and validator repair compare the current policy with only the new guide reservation omitted. Every other field still must match exactly. This projection does not rewrite the saved policy or ledger and cannot raise an earlier allowance.
+13. A unit is `{stage, pageIds, figureId}` plus an optional `task`. Only figure crop review sets it (`figure-crop`); the task joins the unit key, receipt and fingerprint only when present, so existing keys and fingerprints are unchanged. A first attempt may carry `headroomMicrousd`: like a review, it is admitted only when its reservation fits beside every charge, outstanding reservation and that headroom, so discovered work cannot use the reservation of remaining mandatory stages.
+14. Each Meta call's reservation must cover its worst-case charge. Before preparing an attempt, the pipeline prices the counted input plus the framing reserve at the uncached input rate and the full output ceiling at the output rate, using the policy's `metaRates`. If that exceeds the stage reservation, the call fails `limit-exceeded` before any attempt row or charge. Figure crop review reserves the optional `figure-crop` amount; a policy without it uses the figures reservation. Guard admission and validator repair choose this reservation by stage and task. Policies without `metaRates` (historical and offline fixtures) skip the price check and keep their flat reservations.
